#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16601513-9b74-5cec-9af6-2d99b98e1ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "5110af12-1d0a-5f7e-8a3b-626cc437c53e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6097" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4} \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\, \\textrm{d}y$." :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{2}^{4}[∫_{0}^{2}\\frac{x^{3}}{y^{2}}dx]dy &= ∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}[\\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}(\\frac{2^{4}}{4} - \\frac{0^{4}}{4})dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 4∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}dy &= 4[- \\frac{1}{y}]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&= 4(-\\frac{1}{4} - (-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d237f9fb-b5d2-53e0-bc28-ee50d9e08852" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "bd93891e-1f9f-51af-a5c2-4eb5ca1178d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6098" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} \\dfrac y{x^2} \\, \\textrm d A$, where\n$D=\\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\:2 \\leq x \\leq 4,\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 3\\right\\}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{y}{x^{2}}dA &= ∫_{0}^{3}[∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{y}{x^{2}}dx]dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{3}[∫_{2}^{4}\\frac{y}{x^{2}}dx]dy &= ∫_{0}^{3}[- \\frac{y}{x}]_{2}^{4}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}(-\\frac{y}{4} - (-\\frac{y}{2}))dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}\\frac{y}{4}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{3}\\frac{y}{4}dy &= [\\frac{y^{2}}{8}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{2}}{8} - \\frac{0^{2}}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "e51c2aa9-027b-5b23-bcaf-805804a8de9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6099" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$.\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}y \\, \\text{d}x$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{0}^{1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}y\\, \\text{d}x$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}x \\, \\text{d}y$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from ${\\color{blue}0}$ to ${\\color{blue}2}$, and\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from ${\\color{red}0}$ to ${\\color{red}1}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)e^{\\sqrt{xy}}dA &= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}dx]dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{\\color{blue}{2}} \\left[ \\int_{\\color{red}0}^{\\color{red}1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}{\\color{red}y} \\right] \\text{d}{\\color{blue}x}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}]}
